    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so if my LDL is high, is it like it's just sitting there, or is it actively doing something to the artery walls? i'm trying to visualize how the damage actually happens.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question! When LDL cholesterol is high, it can infiltrate the endothelial lining of your arteries, leading to plaque formation. Over time, this plaque can become unstable and lead to a buildup of cholesterol and other substances, causing a thickening of the arterial walls – a condition known as atherosclerosis. As plaque narrows the arteries, it restricts blood flow, potentially leading to blockages, chest pain, heart attack, or stroke.",
      "source": "target",
